# Inequality Mechanisms project rules

- This is scientific research software, not a product prototype.
- Favor correctness, explicitness, and testability over clever abstractions.
- Read `architecture_version` and the relevant ADR before changing graph-state semantics.
- Version 1 noninjective or full-cycle experiments preserve complete actuator-state identity in U.
- Version 2 experiments may use output-state identity in Q only on a certified one-to-one operating branch with a unique actuator realization attached.
- Never collapse duplicate output preimages into one plain Q node.
- Apply shared joint limits and output-coordinate semantics through `OutputSpace`.
- Keep graph topology, planning-state coordinates, actuator realization, sampling provenance, and transition parameterization explicit.
- Do not silently change experiment definitions or mathematical conventions.
- Do not add dependencies without explaining why they are needed.
- Core kinematics, graph construction, objectives, and search algorithms must not live only in notebooks.
- Every implementation task must include tests.
- Dijkstra and A* must use documented node-expansion semantics.
- A* optimal cost must be validated against Dijkstra for every supported objective/heuristic pair.
- Preserve Version 1 golden fixtures during Version 2 rearchitecture.
- Use NumPy-style docstrings and Python type annotations.
- Prefer small, composable modules and compatibility adapters over rename-only rewrites.
- Never modify generated results to make an experiment look better.
- Update or add the relevant ADR when changing a foundational decision.
- Do not implement future-sprint work opportunistically when executing a numbered sprint issue.
